𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗦𝗽𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗿𝗮𝗺𝗲𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸 𝗕𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴𝘀 𝗖𝗿𝗲𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝗮𝗹 𝗗𝗲𝘀𝗶𝗴𝗻 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻𝘀 𝘁𝗼 𝗟𝗶𝗳𝗲! 🚀

If you’re a fan of hashtag#DesignPatterns and use hashtag#SpringFramework, here's something that might surprise you:

👉 Spring uses almost every Creational Design Pattern under the hood — and we use them every day without even realizing it!

Creational patterns abstract the instantiation process and help make a system independent of how its objects are created, composed, and represented. The main ones are:

 • Singleton
 • Factory Method
 • Abstract Factory
 • Builder
 • Prototype

✅ 𝟭. 𝗦𝗶𝗻𝗴𝗹𝗲𝘁𝗼𝗻 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻 – 𝗢𝗻𝗲 𝗕𝗲𝗮𝗻 𝗹𝗶𝘃𝗲𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗴𝗵𝗼𝘂𝘁 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗰𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗲𝘅𝘁

 • In Spring, all beans are Singleton by default (within a container).
 • No need for manual synchronization or getInstance() hacks.

@Component
public class EmailService {
 // Only one instance per context
}

📌 Use Case: Service classes, Repositories, Controllers — Spring manages lifecycle & ensures one shared instance.

✅ 𝟮. 𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆 𝗠𝗲𝘁𝗵𝗼𝗱 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻 – 𝗔𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗦𝗽𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝘁𝗼 𝗖𝗿𝗲𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗜𝘁

 • Using @Bean methods or static factory methods to tell Spring how to create a bean.

@Bean
public DataSource dataSource() {
 return DataSourceBuilder.create()
 .url("jdbc:h2:mem:test")
 .build(); // Factory Method in action!
}

📌 Use Case: Creating complex or configurable beans like DataSource, RestTemplate, WebClient.

✅ 𝟯. 𝗔𝗯𝘀𝘁𝗿𝗮𝗰𝘁 𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻 – 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗕𝗲𝗮𝗻 𝗙𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆 𝗜𝘁𝘀𝗲𝗹𝗳

 • Spring’s ApplicationContext and BeanFactory are textbook Abstract Factories.
 • Ask for a bean by name or type, and Spring decides how to create it.

var context = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);
UserService service = context.getBean(UserService.class);

📌 Use Case: Centralized bean creation across different modules without tight coupling.

✅ 𝟰. 𝗕𝘂𝗶𝗹𝗱𝗲𝗿 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻 – 𝗙𝗹𝘂𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗔𝗣𝗜𝘀 𝗔𝗹𝗹 𝗔𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗻𝗱

 • Spring loves builders — especially in Spring WebFlux, Spring Boot, and Spring Security.

WebClient webClient = WebClient.builder()
 .baseUrl("https://api.example.com")
 .defaultHeader("Authorization", "Bearer token")
 .build();

📌 Use Case: Building immutable, configurable clients, requests, URIs, or application contexts.

Other builders:
 • SpringApplicationBuilder
 • UriComponentsBuilder
 • ServerResponse.ok()... (WebFlux)
 • HttpSecurity in Spring Security

✅ 𝟱. 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝘁𝗼𝘁𝘆𝗽𝗲 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻 – 𝗡𝗲𝘄 𝗕𝗲𝗮𝗻 𝗘𝘃𝗲𝗿𝘆 𝗧𝗶𝗺𝗲

 • Using Prototype scope when needing a fresh instance every time a bean is requested.

@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class TaskProcessor {
 // New object for every injection or retrieval
}

📌 Use Case: Stateless or request-scoped components like job handlers, form objects.

